# Docker / Kubernetes / Terraform coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' style rules to Dockerfiles, Docker Compose files, Kubernetes/Helm YAML and Terraform. Applies only to those file types, not to application source under these directories.

## Dockerfile

- Order instructions from least to most frequently changing (base image, system
  packages, dependency manifests, dependency install, then source copy).
- Multi-stage builds (`FROM ... AS builder`, then a slim final stage copying only
  the built artifact) for any compiled or bundled language.
- Prefer a minimal, maintained base image (`-slim`/`-alpine`, distroless or
  hardened) unless the project needs the extra tooling at runtime.
- Chain related package-manager commands into one `RUN`
  (`apt-get update && apt-get install -y ... && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*`).
- Ship a `.dockerignore` excluding `.git/`, `node_modules/`/`vendor/` and any
  local env/secret file.

## Docker Compose

- Pin every service's `image:` (a tag, or a digest for anything security-sensitive);
  never `image: latest` in a committed compose file.
- Keep secrets and per-environment values in `.env`/`environment:` sourced from the
  shell or a gitignored secrets file, not hardcoded in `docker-compose.yml`.
- Name every custom network and volume explicitly.

## Kubernetes and Helm

- One resource kind's concerns per manifest file where the layout allows (a
  Deployment, its Service, its ConfigMap as separate files or a clearly
  delimited multi-document YAML).
- Set explicit `resources.requests`/`resources.limits` on every container.
- Per-environment values (namespace, hostname, replica count) go in a Helm
  `values.yaml` or Kustomize overlay, not hardcoded in a template.
- Label and select with `app.kubernetes.io/name` and `app.kubernetes.io/instance`.

## Terraform

- Organize a module around `main.tf`/`variables.tf`/`outputs.tf`, plus
  `versions.tf` pinning the provider and Terraform version.
- Name resources and variables for what they are (`aws_db_instance.orders`, not
  `aws_db_instance.db1`); give every variable a `description` and, where one
  exists, a `type` constraint.
- Use a module (`modules/network`, `modules/service`) for any resource group
  reused across environments, not copy-pasted `.tf` blocks.
